The $\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{X}$ bond strength decreases from $\mathrm{HF}$ to HI. i.e. $\mathrm{HF}\gt \mathrm{HCl}\gt \mathrm{HBr}\gt \mathrm{HI}$. Thus $\mathrm{HF}$ is most stable while HI is least stable. The decreasing stability of the hydrogen halide is also reflected in the values of dissociation energy of the $\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{X}$ bond

\begin{array}{cccc}
\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{F} & \mathrm{H}-\mathrm{Cl} & \mathrm{H}-\mathrm{Br} & \mathrm{H}-\mathrm{I} \\
135 \mathrm{kcal} \mathrm{mol}^{-1} & 103 \mathrm{kcal} \mathrm{mol}^{-1} & 87 \mathrm{kcal} \mathrm{mol}^{-1} & 71 \mathrm{kcal} \mathrm{mol}^{-1}
\end{array}

$30 \%$ solution of $\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}_{2}$ is known as perhydrol
$\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposes as

2 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}_{2} \longrightarrow 2 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}+\mathrm{O}_{2}

Volume strength of $30 \% \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}_{2}$ solution is 100 i.e., $1 \mathrm{~mL}$ of this solution on decomposition gives $100 \mathrm{~mL}$ oxygen.
